{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Scope decision while implementing this with `unamused-cloudy-junebug` (9c93-754e-b641-48d1) as one\nchange to `infra/terraform/user_data.sh`.\n\n**F3 — FIXED.** `mount_ebs_volume` now calls a new `replace_fstab_entry`, which backs `/etc/fstab`\nup to `/etc/fstab.rebar-bak` (AC2) and rewrites by MOUNT POINT rather than appending when the new\nUUID is absent (AC1): `awk '$2 != mp'` reads the backup and writes `/etc/fstab`, then the new line\nis appended. The replacement scenario from the ticket now leaves exactly one entry, and an\nunrelated `/` entry survives. Seeded: deleting the `DROP-STALE-FSTAB-ENTRY` line reproduces the\nreported two-entry accumulation exactly, which is what the seed shape was chosen for.\n\n**F4 — FIXED.** `chmod 0700` now precedes both marker writes. Note for the record that the premise\n\"and the runbook\" does not hold: neither `review-bot-ops.md` nor `provision-restore.md` mentions\n`chmod` at all, so there was no documented sequence to correct. AC3's first branch was the cheaper\nand better one, so the script moved.\n\n**F5 — FIXED.** The nvme-cli branch now returns `readlink -f \"$d\"`, matching the by-id fallback, so\nboth branches answer with the same canonical string. Worth recording that the disparity is only\nOBSERVABLE when the node the glob finds is itself a link (the loop devices in the sandbox); on a\nreal Nitro host `/dev/nvme1n1` is a device node and the two branches happened to agree. The test\ntherefore reproduces the sandbox's shape deliberately, or it would have had no teeth.\n\n**F6 — DEFERRED, and the premise is partly wrong.** The claim under audit is\n`variables.tf:63`'s \"Single-sourced here because four things must agree on it — user_data.sh's\nfstab entry, the review-bot's REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR/TMPDIR, observability.sh's df probe, and the\nCloudWatch alarm's `mount` dimension.\"\n\nTwo things the ticket did not account for:\n\n1. That sentence already DOES what AC5's second branch asks for — it names the places that must be\n   updated together, all four of them, by name.\n2. The agreement is not merely asserted in prose: `tests/unit/test_gate_scratch_volume_aa40.py`\n   pins it offline, from a single `SCRATCH_MOUNT` constant, across `variables.tf`,\n   `docker-compose.yml` (both `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` and `TMPDIR`), the compose bind, and\n   `observability.sh`. Changing the terraform default in isolation therefore fails a unit test, not\n   a production boot. The silent desync the ticket describes is already gated.\n\nSo the residue is one imprecise word (\"single-sourced\" for what is really \"duplicated with a\ncommitted agreement test\"), in a file this change otherwise does not touch, whose only real fix —\nderiving the compose value from terraform — is a design decision about templating\n`docker-compose.yml` that belongs in its own ticket rather than bundled into a mount-assertion fix.\n`infra/scripts/observability.sh` is also currently held by another agent.\n\nNot padding this change with it. If the wording still bothers us, it is a one-line doc edit that\nshould be filed separately with the templating question stated properly."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788585001385557001,"uuid":"e6d04f1f-7675-4613-955c-a62848943b1a"}